\b[No.] In the <Gaussian integers>, choose
$$
P=(2+i)\subset\mathbb Z[i],\qquad Q=P\cap\mathbb Z=(5).
$$
Indeed, substituting $i=-2$ gives $\mathbb Z[i]/(2+i)\cong\mathbb Z/5\mathbb Z$, so $P$ is a <prime ideal> and its contraction is $(5)$. Put $\beta=2-i$. Modulo $P$, $\beta$ becomes $4\ne0$ in $\mathbb F_5$, so $\beta\notin P$. Therefore
$$
\alpha=\frac1{2-i}=\frac{2+i}{5}\in\mathbb Z[i]_P.
$$
We show that $\alpha$ is not an <integral element> over $\mathbb Z_{(5)}$, using a direct reduction argument. Write $B=\mathbb Z_{(5)}[i]$, viewed inside $\mathbb Q(i)$. There is a <ring homomorphism>
$$
\rho:B\longrightarrow\mathbb F_5,\qquad i\longmapsto2,
$$
which reduces rational coefficients modulo $5$. It is well defined because denominators in $\mathbb Z_{(5)}$ are not divisible by $5$, and $2^2+1=0$ in $\mathbb F_5$. In particular, $\rho(\beta)=0$ and $\rho(1)=1$.

If $\alpha$ satisfied a <monic> equation over $\mathbb Z_{(5)}$, say
$$
\alpha^n+a_{n-1}\alpha^{n-1}+\cdots+a_0=0,
$$
multiplication by $\beta^n$ would give an equality in $B$:
$$
1+a_{n-1}\beta+a_{n-2}\beta^2+\cdots+a_0\beta^n=0.
$$
Applying $\rho$ gives $1=0$ in $\mathbb F_5$, a contradiction. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbb Z[i]_{(2+i)}\text{ is not integral over }\mathbb Z_{(5)}.}
$$
This exhibits why <localization of an integral extension at a prime need not be integral>. Localizing both <rings> by the same <multiplicative subset> preserves an <integral extension>, but $\mathbb Z[i]_P$ inverts additional elements, including $2-i$, whose image at the other <prime ideal> $(2-i)$ is zero. The original extension is integral because $i$ satisfies the <monic> equation $T^2+1=0$: the <subalgebra generated by finitely many integral elements> property applies to $\mathbb Z[i]$.
